WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
How do you see one cell inside a crowded slice of brain?

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:08.960
Stain every cell at once, and you get a dark crowd of bodies and tangled threads.

3
00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.540
No eye can follow one.

4
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:17.633
Instead, biologists stain one target at a time.

5
00:00:18.953 --> 00:00:23.373
A Nissl stain binds the RNA-rich cytoplasm around each nucleus.

6
00:00:24.953 --> 00:00:29.553
Cell bodies light up across the section, good for counting cells and finding layer

7
00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:30.553
boundaries.

8
00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:34.557
Nissl maps cell bodies, yet branches stay hidden.

9
00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:38.297
A Golgi stain solves the opposite problem.

10
00:00:39.557 --> 00:00:44.497
It fills a small, unpredictable fraction of cells completely, including their

11
00:00:44.497 --> 00:00:45.497
branching dendrites.

12
00:00:46.857 --> 00:00:50.137
Against a clear background, each stained neuron stands out whole.

13
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:55.187
To ask which cells carry one chosen protein, use immunostaining.

14
00:00:56.487 --> 00:01:01.487
An antibody binds that protein, marking only cells where the target was detected.

15
00:01:02.807 --> 00:01:06.747
A stain reveals only its target, so match the stain to your question.

16
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:14.310
Now test a limit. Under the best light microscope, points closer than about 200

17
00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:15.870
nanometres blur into one.

18
00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:21.390
The gap between two cells at a chemical synapse is about 20 nanometres.

19
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.330
If two stained branches look joined, can this stain prove they fuse?

20
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.920
No. Divide 200 nanometres by 20 nanometres, and you get ten.

21
00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.600
So the gap is about ten times below the limit.

22
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:48.380
A stained section can be 100 micrometres or more thick, stacking branches above and

23
00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:49.380
below.

24
00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:51.443
Binding can mislead, too.

25
00:01:52.663 --> 00:01:57.483
An antibody against a mouse protein may miss the chick version, or fail when its

26
00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:04.323
site is masked. In 1994, Smerdu and colleagues studied human fibres long labelled

27
00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:05.763
type IIb by staining.

28
00:02:07.023 --> 00:02:12.203
Those fibres express mostly type IIx myosin, not the rodent type IIb form.

29
00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:15.643
So the stain's label named the wrong protein.

30
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:19.627
Used with care, stains unlocked discoveries.

31
00:02:20.987 --> 00:02:25.407
In 1873, the Golgi stain filled just a few neurons completely.

32
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:31.767
That turned separate cells versus a fused network into a question argued with

33
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:36.987
evidence. Settling the gap still took about eighty years and the electron

34
00:02:36.987 --> 00:02:37.987
microscope.

35
00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:45.637
In 1884, Hans Christian Gram published a stain sorting bacteria by their envelopes.

36
00:02:46.697 --> 00:02:49.257
Cells take up a purple dye, then an alcohol wash.

37
00:02:50.577 --> 00:02:54.677
A thick peptidoglycan wall holds the dye, so those cells stay purple.

38
00:02:56.217 --> 00:02:59.757
Thin-walled cells lose it, and a red counterstain turns them pink.

39
00:03:01.057 --> 00:03:06.517
Yet some archaea lack peptidoglycan and stain purple: it reads the envelope, not the

40
00:03:06.517 --> 00:03:07.517
domain.

41
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:12.810
Nicole Le Douarin used a DNA stain to track migrating cells.

42
00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:19.010
Quail nuclei show a dense chromatin mass by the nucleolus that chick nuclei lack.

43
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:25.010
Grafted quail cells keep that mark in chick sections without fading or diluting.

44
00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.590
Across every tissue, a stain reports only what it binds.
